WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.009 --> 00:00:10.350
The overview: who’s listening right now, with the book, the chapter and how far through.

2
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:17.238
Health checks run on every scan: duplicates, missing covers, books that aren’t matched yet.

3
00:00:17.238 --> 00:00:20.937
Search is everywhere. Press ⌘K and type.

4
00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:27.576
This one has a problem. Its title is <b>Bernard Cornwell</b> and its author is <b>Sharpe’s Eagle</b>.

5
00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:36.069
The tags are swapped and there’s no cover. Each field shows where its value came from.

6
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:42.582
But the folders say <b>Bernard Cornwell / Richard Sharpe / Sharpe - 08 - Sharpe’s Eagle</b>.

7
00:00:42.582 --> 00:00:47.909
Match looks it up in the community database, folder names and all.

8
00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:55.297
The right book comes first, with its cover, and each edition’s length is checked against the files.

9
00:00:55.297 --> 00:01:03.663
Then field by field. It had no cover, so the community one is ticked too.

10
00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:10.051
Fixed. The files aren’t touched: it’s stored as overrides that a rescan won’t undo.

11
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:15.578
The author, narrator and series are links to the rest of their books.

12
00:01:18.503 --> 00:01:25.898
The library opens on what’s new, and on books that could do with some attention.

13
00:01:25.898 --> 00:01:36.196
Filters for everything the server knows: format, codec, cover, chapters, matched or not.

14
00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:46.695
A library can take book details from folder names instead, for folders named better than they’re tagged.

15
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:54.772
Community data puts each series in order. A complete one gets a full shelf.

16
00:01:54.772 --> 00:02:01.799
Books you don’t have yet show up as gaps on the shelf.

17
00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:10.392
One folder, one book, unless I say otherwise. These Narnia files are seven separate books, and rescans remember.

18
00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:21.727
Health turns scan results into a to-do list. Unmatched books can be looked up all at once.

19
00:02:21.727 --> 00:02:31.963
The server does it in the background. Nothing changes until I’ve reviewed it.

20
00:02:31.963 --> 00:02:39.006
I choose what gets written: just the IDs, what’s missing, or the community’s values.

21
00:02:39.006 --> 00:02:46.861
Less certain ones wait for a closer look, with each book’s folder and the match’s series.

22
00:02:46.861 --> 00:02:53.112
Scans run in a queue, on a schedule per library, with a log for each run.

23
00:02:56.156 --> 00:03:02.295
Everyone on the server, what they’re in the middle of, and who’s listening now.

24
00:03:02.295 --> 00:03:14.507
Each person gets their own year in listening, and a list of every device they’ve signed in on.

25
00:03:14.507 --> 00:03:25.110
Adding someone is an invite: a link or a QR code they scan with the app. No password needed.

26
00:03:28.291 --> 00:03:35.729
Activity: hours, sessions, books finished. All on the server, no third-party analytics.

27
00:03:35.729 --> 00:03:45.392
When the household listens, which apps they use, and what needed converting to play.

28
00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:51.636
And a year in listening for the whole server.

29
00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:02.511
Settings live in the console. The apps know the home address and the public one, and switch on their own.

30
00:04:02.511 --> 00:04:08.463
Moving from Audiobookshelf? Import brings everyone’s listening history over.

31
00:04:08.463 --> 00:04:19.655
Scheduled database backups, and alerts to a webhook, ntfy or Discord.

32
00:04:19.655 --> 00:04:28.547
Live logs, and an audit log of every change an admin makes.

33
00:04:31.212 --> 00:04:34.976
Light or dark, or it follows your system.
